      "text": "To solve this problem, we can use the principle of counting with restrictions. We need to color the hexagon with the R red and then count the number of ways to color the remaining hexagons while ensuring no two neighboring hexagons have the same color.\n\nSteps:\n1. Consider the hexagon with the R colored in. This hexagon can be colored red (1 way).\n2. The neighboring hexagons (missing the R) have 2 possible colors (yellow or green) for each one.\n3. The next layer (three gaps) can have 2 color choices.\n4. The pattern continues with 2 choices for each interior hexagon.\n\nHence, the totality of ways to color the hexagons is $2^7$.\n\nThus, the number of different ways to color the figure is $2^7=\\boxed{128}$.",
