      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Instead of thinking about rotation, let us try to find the pattern of colors:\n\\begin{align*}\nR, &\\; Y, \\color{red}{R}, \\; G, \\; Y, \\color{red}{R} \\\\\n\\color{red}{R}, &\\; R, \\; Y, \\; G, \\; Y, \\color{red}{R} \\\\\nY, &\\; G, \\color{red}{R}, \\; Y, \\, \\color{red}{R}, \\; R \\\\\n\\color{red}{Y}, &\\; \\color{red}{Y}, \\, G, \\color{red}{R}, \\; Y, \\; R \\\\\nG, &\\; R, \\color{red}{Y}, \\; Y, \\color{red}{G}, \\; Y, \\color{red}{R} \\\\\n\\color{red}{G}, &\\; \\color{red}{G}, \\, R, \\color{red}{Y}, \\; Y, \\color{red}{G}, \\; G \\\\\n\\end{align*}\nThis pattern repeats every six hexagons.    We can put the figure on a hex grid, like a checkerboard, where the R hexagon is at a white/white coordinate, while the other hexagons are all filled with red.  So we must, e.g., fill in the first five black, and then count the number of ways to fill in the next three.  The other 6 numbers are from the same pattern, in those black hashtable.  The total number of different ways is thus $5\\cdot6=30.$",
